WEBVTT

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:05.520
Don't worry, I won't ever have to go to the Basilica myself.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.230
Say "Hi" to Yujin for me.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:20.620
Joan...

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.370
You're not expecting me to actually show up, are you?

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.040
"Morosov said, as he pointed to the wooden Madonna,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.460
'This isn't a thank you, it's a cry for help.'"

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.680
I lived, yet you disappeared.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.900
Let me guess. You're reading "Arc de Triomphe" again, right?

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.600
Geez, you're going to memorize the entire book at this rate.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.560
My father liked it.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.820
Apparently, he used to ask Mom to read it to him every day.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.530
Don't you miss your dad?

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.030
You think I'm a kid or something?

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.950
I may be an adult, but I still miss my dad.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.620
Who did this?

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:17.880
I'm sorry, Sister.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.650
I'll have it restored to precisely the way it used to look.

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:24.510
How?

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.560
I'll find a way.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.640
It'd take another "Miracle of the Basilica"

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.900
to put that broken glass back together again.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.650
You no longer have any right or responsibility to work here.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.350
Claude will be here soon.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.570
Whether he chooses to pay for the glass or restore it

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.740
is no longer any of your concern.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.200
But Sister...

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.580
I...

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.090
Why don't you guys take the day off?

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.260
Today's the first day of the festival. Go out there and have some fun.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.260
Really?

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.230
Don't worry. It'll all be okay.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.400
Where are you going?

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.110
To meet Marianne.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:26.160
I hope she's the one, since Claude's going to be here now.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.660
Sister Catherine?

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.960
Miracles <i>do</i> happen, don't they?

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.750
I sure could use one right now.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.560
I think this one would look better on you.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:49.980
Why don't you try it on?

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:54.160
I'm taking the picture now.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.370
That's good.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:07.590
I'm taking another one.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.450
Hey, Louis! Louis!

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:41.580
Watch out!

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:44.250
Mommy!

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.470
Are you all right, Yujin?

00:06:59.160 --> 00:06:59.820
Got you!

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:01.810
Are you all right?

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:02.520
You're bleeding.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.860
Bleeding? Where?

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.840
You made a big deal out of this...

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.550
"A big deal," you say?

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.970
We were going to renovate the window frames anyway.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:41.810
I guess we'll have to compensate you for the damaged stained glass.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.980
Of course, any compensation will have to be paid only after

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.780
we've tried our hand at restoring it to its original appearance.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.820
Is that all you have to say?

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:53.820
That takes care of the business end of my visit,

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.740
and I can't say I'm a fan of festivals.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.250
I'm thinking of leaving right now.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.460
If I remember correctly, you gave your word that

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:06.700
you'd take charge of the construction yourself,

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:09.050
if things got even a little out of hand.

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:11.000
Who said that?

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.090
I did?

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.590
Nothing in the contract implied that.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.350
You dare bring filth into the house of the Lord by deceiving us

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.850
with matters as trifling as details in a contract?

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:27.310
I'm in charge of the construction here, even now.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:31.190
I just happen to have someone here in my place, that's all.

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:36.490
If you'll excuse me.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.670
And you can't possibly blame me for something

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:49.340
a stray gypsy kid did.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.890
But the one who let him into the chapel was—

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:57.220
Playing the pipe organ in the middle of the night, huh?

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.050
Did somebody take up a new hobby?

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:23.160
"Morosov said, as he pointed to the wooden Madonna,

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:27.120
'This isn't a thank you, it's a cry for help.'"

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:40.600
Joan...

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.100
Why is this book here?

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:50.400
This book?

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.070
Whose book is this?

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:54.280
It's Louis'...

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:55.070
Louis?

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:56.320
Where is he now?

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:04.410
Then...

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:09.290
you don't remember anything about it at all?

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.170
Sorry I couldn't be of any help,

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:14.300
but according to the regulations we had at the asylum

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:19.800
patients could not be tended to by nurses of the opposite sex

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:23.810
who were close to the age of the patient.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:25.310
I see.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:28.450
We did have a few volunteer workers,

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:30.190
but they were all middle aged.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.440
Maybe she's looking for Joan?

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:34.730
Joan?

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:39.360
<i>Fate is often cruel, you know?</i>

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:50.960
To Joan.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.080
Oh yeah!

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.680
You were also a patient at the Basilica's asylum back then, weren't you?

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:07.020
I've been in and out of asylums ever since my legs became useless.

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.060
Do you remember anyone by the name of Claude?

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.770
You don't have to shout. My ears are still fine.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:18.190
I'm sorry.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:21.280
I don't remember a Claude,

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.550
but there was this one fellow who

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.950
followed Joan around everywhere she went.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:29.080
Are you sure her name was "Joan"?

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:32.750
I didn't see a "Joan" on the list.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:34.130
What list?

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:36.340
The list of nurses who worked at the asylum.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.130
What are you talking about?

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.630
Joan was a patient.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.510
A patient?

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:55.060
Who's there?

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:57.980
Who are you?

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.280
That's an interesting hobby you have there.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:01.120
Excuse me?

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.660
You read books upside down?

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:07.280
I'm not really reading.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.540
Then why are you holding a book?

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.960
So the others will think I'm reading.

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:17.630
Why?

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.100
I'd rather look like a madman who's reading a book upside-down,

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:23.470
than a blind man.

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:27.600
The author would be sad if he heard that.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:31.640
Here, I'll read it for you.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:33.830
Are you a nurse here?

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:35.650
Or are you one of the volunteers?

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:37.650
I'm Joan.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:39.440
Joan?

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:42.650
I guess that'd make me Ravic.

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:45.410
Where should I start?

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:47.660
Wherever you want.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:56.330
"Ravic sat at the table close to the exit with the young woman.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:00.880
He liked sitting by the exit since it made escaping easier when he had to.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:03.100
'Two glasses of Calvados, please,'

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:05.680
he told the garçon."

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:09.760
Only a few of us knew about this,

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:13.270
but in front of that guy, Joan acted as if she could see,

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:15.390
while she was actually losing her vision.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.900
Sometimes we'd help her sound more convincing.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:20.690
Why did she do it?

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.400
Was she just playing with him?

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:24.050
Playing with him?

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:25.900
No, he was different.

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.310
He had given up all hope for himself,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.200
despite being young and having his whole life ahead of him.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:34.750
But thanks to Joan, he started to change, little by little.

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:39.000
How did Joan read the book to him if she couldn't see either?

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:43.250
Joan loved reading.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:49.800
"Morosov said, as he pointed to the wooden Madonna,

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:54.470
'This isn't a thank you, it's a cry for help.'"

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:00.020
That's my favorite line from the book.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:03.940
Did you see that I underlined it?

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:06.650
Yeah, I did.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:10.660
Back then I could see out of my own two eyes.

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.240
Why don't we stop here for today?

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:22.430
How do you like it?

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:23.800
Pretty sloppy, right?

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:25.960
I tried to make a good one for you.

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:30.550
No, it looks just like the statue of the Madonna out in the garden.

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:33.560
I decided to have the operation.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:36.100
It'll be my third time trying it,

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:38.060
and I really don't want to go again,

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:39.730
but I'm going to try anyway,

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.900
for you.

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:44.570
I'm glad you're doing it. {literal translation: (that's) good thinking}

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:47.850
Even if the operation is a failure,

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:50.070
and I still can't see afterwards...

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.030
Just try to look on the bright side of it.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.700
Make a better carving for me

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:59.000
if the operation is successful, okay?

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:01.460
I will.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:05.300
You want to leave this place?

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:07.010
Leave?

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:14.930
Don't let go of my hand, okay?

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:26.820
What if the Head Sister finds out?

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:30.320
The festival only takes place one day of the year, you know.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:31.820
What should we drink?

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:35.660
Two glasses of Calvados, please.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:40.540
I can't believe we're actually out here drinking Calvados.

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:44.250
Yeah, I feel like I'm Ravic.

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.250
I'm glad we left the asylum.

00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:51.300
Let's make a toast.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:53.470
To Joan.

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:09.530
It's all in the past now.

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:14.700
I probably wouldn't have remembered it, if it weren't for you guys.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.160
Hey Gramps, what was Joan's real name?

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:21.950
Tell me!

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.210
What's wrong, Louis?

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:29.300
Joan's name is...

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:30.590
It's "Anne," isn't it?

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:32.220
Isn't it?

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.220
How did you know?

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:41.720
Monsieur Claude.

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:09.210
What would you like?

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:12.460
Two glasses of Calvados, please.

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:15.010
Are you waiting for someone?

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:18.140
Don't you see the gentleman

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:21.640
sitting next to me?

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:25.350
It's been a while since we came here, hasn't it, Ravic?

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:42.910
Why don't we mix some mulled cider into it?

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:46.750
Calvados is too strong for us to be drinking straight on our reunion.

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:54.260
It's still as lousy as before, isn't it?

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:02.640
It's just like the statue of the Madonna out in the garden.

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:03.770
Is it?

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:05.770
Oh, Ravic...

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:11.770
I missed you.

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:18.700
I missed you too.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:34.800
Let me see.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:39.720
I want to see you.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:18:59.320
You're fired.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.660
You wanted to "pour your heart" into something you're building, right?

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:05.450
You wanted to learn to pour your heart and "engrave your memories"

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:07.250
into something you're building, didn't you?

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:12.540
Over the last two years, I've taught you everything I know,

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:15.040
but I only realized today,

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:19.800
that everything I've done up 'til now has been mere mimicry.

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:24.470
This entire time, I've been running away

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:27.010
from the Basilica that's been waiting for me so dearly.

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:31.770
How could I peer into the memories that have been engraved in this place,

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:35.570
or even expect to pour my heart into it, when I've been running from it?

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:38.190
Monsieur...

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:42.240
Perhaps the only thing I can truly teach you

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:45.030
is the fact that I've been wrong this whole time.

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:48.450
Don't make the same mistakes I've made.

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.000
Don't run away anymore.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.420
If there are tangled strings in your life, untie them.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:58.840
What if the tangled strings don't want to come undone?

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:04.300
If you can't untie them, cutting them is an alternative.

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:08.390
That's the only way you can truly move on.

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:10.100
How can I...

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:14.810
You found Joan for me.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:18.150
Leave this place to me. I'll make sure it's taken care of.

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:41.590
I'll write. I promise.

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:44.590
You're going back to Korea, then?

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:48.110
I need to look for someone before I do that.

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:50.600
Yujin!

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.100
Here, I found it.

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:55.230
Found what?

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:57.100
This.

00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:00.610
Remember you had a cassette tape in that bag I stole?

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:07.490
Back then, I wondered what it was, so I played it in this thing

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:10.240
and forgot to take it out after I was done.

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:12.290
I thought I'd lost it.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:17.630
I looked for it many times, even after I returned your bag to you.

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:20.960
But I couldn't find it because someone was using it.

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:25.640
I searched the entire camp for this when I heard you were leaving.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:27.970
You have no idea how hard I looked for it.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:29.860
What is it?

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:31.140
It must be important.

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:32.930
Want to listen to it?

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:34.490
Yeah.

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:44.400
<i>Surprised?</i>

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:46.400
<i>I know it's late, but I got you a Christmas present.</i>

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:48.570
<i>Merry Christmas!</i>

00:21:56.910 --> 00:21:58.050
Who is it?

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.880
Is that the man you're looking for?

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:04.780
Yeah.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:11.390
Y-Yujin...

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:12.990
<i>Why are you crying, Yujin?</i>

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:14.560
<i>Are you all right?</i>

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.160
Man, this weather is depressing.

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:33.950
And here I was hoping we could go out together later, Mom.

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:39.420
It's really pouring, isn't it?

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:42.710
A date on a rainy day is much better than on a sunny one.

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:44.750
Is it?

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:48.670
Hey, Mom, you want to go out and drink some sweet rice liquor with me?

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:50.380
Sweet rice liquor?

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:54.270
Come on, I'm an adult now, Mom.

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:57.480
And it's customary to learn how to drink alcohol from your elders, right?

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:01.750
Then how about we go watch a movie?

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:03.730
Is anything good playing right now?

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:06.690
There was this one movie I really wanted to watch with you.

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:09.900
Mail.

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:16.070
It's from Yujin, isn't it?

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:17.240
Show me!

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:19.790
<i>How are you, Mom?</i>

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:23.250
<i>I hope you're not spending your days sighing and worrying about me.</i>

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:26.840
<i>You better not be giving Mom any trouble</i>

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:28.460
<i>and causing her more wrinkles, Huijin.</i>

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:31.180
Look who's talking.

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:34.180
<i>I'm at the airport right now.</i>

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:37.470
<i>I'm heading over to New York on urgent business.</i>

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:39.480
<i>New York is...</i>
